<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Arun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Temple of Dawn, Wat Arun Ratchawararam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Arun, the Temple of Dawn, is a Buddhist temple on the west bank of the Chao Phraya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in the old capital district of Thonburi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> made it his royal temple, and its great central prang, more than 70 metres high, was raised under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> and covered with fragments of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>. It is one of the best-known landmarks of Bangkok.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-arun.jpg" alt="Wat Arun" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mastertongapollo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A Buddhist temple stood on the site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> period, before the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a>, and was known as Wat Bang Makok, later shortened to Wat Makok after the village in which it was built. According to the historian Prince Damrong Rajanubhab, French maps drawn during Narai's reign (1656-88) show it. After the fall of Ayutthaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> (1767-82) founded his new capital at Thonburi beside the temple and renamed it Wat Chaeng. He is believed to have vowed to restore it after passing it at dawn.</p><p>The temple stood within the grounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>'s royal palace and enshrined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a> until the image was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-kaew">Wat Phra Kaew</a> on the eastern bank of the river in 1785. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> (1782-1809) moved the palace across the river, and the temple was abandoned until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a> (1809-24) had it restored and began plans to raise the main pagoda to 70 metres. Work began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> (1824-51), and the main prang was finished in 1851 after nine years of construction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god Aruna, the charioteer of the sun god Surya. The central prang is encrusted with coloured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and with shells of Mauritia mauritiana. The porcelain had earlier served as ballast in boats coming to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Four smaller satellite prang stand at the corners, and the satellite prang is devoted to the wind god Phra Phai. The central prang is topped with a seven-pronged trident, and statues of the Hindu god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> riding on Erawan stand over its second terrace.</p><p>The central prang stands for Mount Meru in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> cosmology, and in Buddhist iconography it has three symbolic levels, the base for the Traiphum, the middle for Tavatimsa and the top for Devaphum. Beside it is the ordination hall, whose Buddha image was supposedly designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a>, whose murals were made under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Rama V</a> and whose entrance is guarded by two demons from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakien">Ramakien</a>, the white Sahassa Deja and the green Thotsakan, who is Ravana. The prang was restored in 1980 and again from 2013 to 2017, when lime plaster replaced the cement of earlier work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wat Arun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally the early kings rebuilt Ayutthayan court culture: the Ramakien, the Thai version of the Ramayana, was compiled under Rama I, the poet Sunthorn Phu composed Phra Aphai Mani in the early nineteenth century, and temples such as Wat Pho and <b>Wat Arun</b> gave Bangkok its skyline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
